<Routines>
<Routine Name="MainRoutine" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[JSR(SequenceRoutine,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="SequenceRoutine" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Sequence V3.0

Please note that this version is dramatically different than previous versions of Sequences. Please consult the below comments and the AIC Wiki for more details.

AIC's default Finite State Machine based loosely on the AB Equipment Phase.
One Shot (ONS) and Latch On (OTL) the command bits (sequence.cmd___) to change states. The sequence controller will unlatch bits when done and signal once it has reached a new state.

<Comments enclosed in brackets intended to be deleted. These comments are mostly a "tutorial" for how to start with this routine. The "clean" version of this routine exludes all tutorial type comments and the stats AOI. The clean version is likely easier to import and use once you are used to this program structure/interface.>

Step Numbering Scheme
-------------------------
Running: 0-1999
Complete: 2000
Pausing: 3000-3999
Paused: 4000
Restarting (Resume from Paused): 5000-5999
Aborting: 6000-6999
Aborted: 7000

<Optional: Delete this rung when done reading>

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[//////////   Prescan   /////////

<
Put code that runs before the sequence here
This is also a good spot for code related to this sequence that runs all the time
Also also this is a good spot to put logic for when to abort or pause, say if your safety trips. Note, DO NOT set the step number manually. ALWAYS use the sequence.cmd___ bits
>]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   Sequence Status and Control   /////////

<
Note: the "mode" should typically be connected to the system mode (1/True for Auto)
"disableHmiStart" should typically be linked to safety such that an Estop disables starting the program
Alternatively, the signals can be left as is to always enable PLC and HMI control of this program regradless of mode or safety or whatever else

For step control:
Enable autoStep to run code normally (auto increment steps)
Disable autoStep and manually toggle singleStep for control of sequence step increments
>
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[SeqCtrl(sequence,1,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   Running   /////////

Steps 0 - 1999

<
Set On the cmdStart bit to reach this step from complete, aborted, or paused

Put your code to do stuff here. Spread out steps so intermediate steps can be added later. 
Always start a step with EQU(seq.currentStep, <some number>) and always end with MOV(<some number>, seq.nextStep)
NEVER write directly to seq.currentStep

Typically, sequences should start with resetting anything needed to run correctly, and then run whatever sequences is required

Go to Complete (Step 2000) when done running or reloop back to an earlier step to run forever
>]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQ(sequence.currentStep,0)MOVE(2000,sequence.nextStep);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   COMPLETE   /////////

Step 2000. Done. Waiting for start command to go back to beginning of running. 
Step transition handled by SeqCtrl AOI.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   Pausing (Holding)   /////////

<
Set on the cmdPause bit when running to reach this point.

OPTIONAL: Stop anything that needs to stop before pausing. SeqCtrl will log what the running step was before pausing. Go to paused (step 4000) when done.
If Pausing/Paused/Restart is not needed, DELETE these steps and disable the Pause button on the HMI.
>]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQ(sequence.currentStep,3000)MOVE(4000,sequence.nextStep);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   Paused  /////////

OPTIONAL
Step 4000. Program paused/held in place. Waiting for start to go resume running where it left off
Step transition handled by SeqCtrl AOI

<If Pausing/Paused/Restart is not needed, DELETE these steps and disable the Pause button on the HMI>]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   Restarting   /////////

<
Set on the cmdStart bit when paused to reach this point.

OPTIONAL: Reset and decide how to resume running, then return to where ever the program was before it was paused.
If Pausing/Paused/Restart is not needed, DELETE these steps and disable the Pause button on the HMI.
>]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQ(sequence.currentStep,5000)MOVE(sequence.runningStepBeforePause,sequence.nextStep);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   ABORTING   /////////

<
Set on the cmdAbort bit to reach this point
Stop some things and then go to aborted (step 7000) when done.
>]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQ(sequence.currentStep,6000)MOVE(7000,sequence.nextStep);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[/////////   Aborted   /////////

Step 7000. Program Stopped/Canceled. Waiting for start to go back to beginning of running.
Step transition handled by SeqCtrl AOI]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
</Routines>
</Program>
